## 📌 Q3. Smallest Difference Between Adjacent Elements

### 🎯 Problem
Find the smallest difference between neighboring elements in a sorted array.

### 💻 Code
```java
int arr[] = {1, 3, 7, 8, 12};

int left = 0;
int right = 1;
int smallest = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

while (right < arr.length) {
    int difference = arr[right] - arr[left];

    if (difference < smallest) {
        smallest = difference;
    }

    left++;
    right++;
}

System.out.println("Smallest Difference = " + smallest);
```

### Output
```text
Smallest Difference = 1
```

### Dry Run

| Pair | Difference |
|---|---:|
| 1, 3 | 2 |
| 3, 7 | 4 |
| 7, 8 | 1 |
| 8, 12 | 4 |

The smallest difference is `1`.

**Time:** O(n)  
**Extra Space:** O(1)

**Interview Tip:** This solution checks adjacent elements, not every possible pair.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 📌 Q4. Check Whether an Array Is a Palindrome

### 🎯 Problem
Check whether the array reads the same forward and backward.

### 💻 Code
```java
int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 2, 1};

int left = 0;
int right = arr.length - 1;
boolean palindrome = true;

while (left < right) {
    if (arr[left] != arr[right]) {
        palindrome = false;
        break;
    }

    left++;
    right--;
}

System.out.println(palindrome);
```

### Output
```text
true
```

### Dry Run
- Compare `1` and `1` → match.
- Compare `2` and `2` → match.
- The pointers meet → palindrome.

**Time:** O(n)  
**Extra Space:** O(1)

**Memory Trick:** Compare the outside elements, then move inward.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 📌 Q5. Remove Duplicates from a Sorted Array

### 🎯 Problem
Move unique elements to the beginning of the array and print them.

### 💻 Code
```java
int arr[] = {1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4};

int left = 0;
int right = 1;

while (right < arr.length) {
    if (arr[right] != arr[left]) {
        left++;
        arr[left] = arr[right];
    }

    right++;
}

System.out.println("Unique elements:");

for (int i = 0; i <= left; i++) {
    System.out.print(arr[i] + " ");
}
```

### Output
```text
Unique elements:
1 2 3 4
```

### Dry Run
- `left` points to the last unique element.
- `right` searches for the next different element.
- When a different element is found, move `left` forward and copy it.

**Time:** O(n)  
**Extra Space:** O(1)

**Important:** This works because the array is sorted. It changes the original array.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 📌 Q6. Move All Zeroes to the End

### 🎯 Problem
Move all non-zero elements to the front while keeping their order, then fill the remaining positions with zeroes.

### 💻 Code
```java
int arr[] = {0, 1, 0, 3, 12};

int left = 0;
int right = 0;

while (right < arr.length) {
    if (arr[right] != 0) {
        arr[left] = arr[right];
        left++;
    }

    right++;
}

for (int i = left; i < arr.length; i++) {
    arr[i] = 0;
}

for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    System.out.print(arr[i] + " ");
}
```

### Output
```text
1 3 12 0 0
```

### Dry Run
First loop places non-zero values at the front:

`1 3 12 3 12`

Second loop fills the remaining positions with zeroes:

`1 3 12 0 0`

**Time:** O(n)  
**Extra Space:** O(1)

**Common Mistake:** The first loop alone does not clear the old values at the end.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 📌 Q7. Reverse an Array In-Place

### 🎯 Problem
Reverse an array without creating another array.

### 💻 Code
```java
int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

int left = 0;
int right = arr.length - 1;

while (left < right) {
    int temp = arr[left];
    arr[left] = arr[right];
    arr[right] = temp;

    left++;
    right--;
}

System.out.print("Reverse = ");

for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    System.out.print(arr[i] + " ");
}
```

### Output
```text
Reverse = 5 4 3 2 1
```

### Dry Run
- Swap `1` and `5` → `5 2 3 4 1`
- Swap `2` and `4` → `5 4 3 2 1`
- Pointers meet at the middle.

**Time:** O(n)  
**Extra Space:** O(1)

**Memory Trick:** Save one value in `temp` before swapping.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 📌 Q8. Check Whether Two Strings Are Anagrams

### 🎯 Problem
Two strings are anagrams if they contain the same characters with the same frequencies, in a different order.

### 💻 Code
```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class AnagramCheck {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s1 = "listen";
        String s2 = "silent";

        if (s1.length() != s2.length()) {
            System.out.println("Not an Anagram");
        } else {
            char[] a = s1.toCharArray();
            char[] b = s2.toCharArray();

            Arrays.sort(a);
            Arrays.sort(b);

            if (Arrays.equals(a, b)) {
                System.out.println("Anagram");
            } else {
                System.out.println("Not an Anagram");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Output
```text
Anagram
```

### Dry Run
- `listen` → sorted characters: `eilnst`
- `silent` → sorted characters: `eilnst`
- Both sorted arrays match.

**Time:** O(n log n)  
**Extra Space:** O(n)

**Important:** This version is case-sensitive and treats spaces as characters.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 📌 Q9. Two Sum — Return Indices

### 🎯 Problem
Find the indices of two different elements whose sum equals the target.

### 💻 Code
```java
int arr[] = {2, 7, 11, 15};
int target = 9;

for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    for (int j = i + 1; j < arr.length; j++) {
        int sum = arr[i] + arr[j];

        if (sum == target) {
            System.out.println("Indices: " + i + ", " + j);
        }
    }
}
```

### Output
```text
Indices: 0, 1
```

### Dry Run
- `arr[0] + arr[1] = 2 + 7 = 9`
- Target found → print indices `0, 1`.

**Time:** O(n²)  
**Extra Space:** O(1)

**Interview Tip:** This nested-loop approach works for unsorted arrays. A HashMap can improve the usual Two Sum solution to O(n) expected time.

